    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete shutdown', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ndelete shutdown\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, in Juniper JUNOS, interfaces are enabled by default, so simply removing any shutdown configuration would achieve the same effect as \"no shutdown\" in Cisco. The equivalent would be to ensure there\\'s no \"disable\" statement under the interface configuration. \\n\\nIf you specifically need a positive enable command in Juniper, it would be:\\n##\\nset disable\\n##\\n\\nBut note this would actually disable the interface (opposite of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\"). The most accurate translation is to simply remove any shutdown/disable statement, as shown in the first example.', 'trans_template': 'delete shutdown'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ndelete shutdown\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, in Juniper JUNOS, interfaces are enabled by default, so simply removing any shutdown configuration would achieve the same effect as \"no shutdown\" in Cisco. The equivalent would be to ensure there\\'s no \"disable\" statement under the interface configuration. \\n\\nIf you specifically need a positive enable command in Juniper, it would be:\\n##\\nset disable\\n##\\n\\nBut note this would actually disable the interface (opposite of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\"). The most accurate translation is to simply remove any shutdown/disable statement, as shown in the first example.', 'trans_template': 'set disable'}]]": 1,
