    "['ethernet cfm [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols oam ethernet connectivity-fault-management maintenance-domain md1 maintenance-association ma1 mep 1 remote-mep 2', 'parent_command': ['set protocols oam ethernet connectivity-fault-management domain md1 level 7', 'set protocols oam ethernet connectivity-fault-management domain md1 association ma1 interface vlan.2'], 'root': 'set protocols oam ethernet connectivity-fault-management domain md1 level 7', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Juniper command for `ethernet cfm crosscheck`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols oam ethernet connectivity-fault-management maintenance-domain md1 maintenance-association ma1 mep 1 remote-mep 2\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\nThe Cisco `ethernet cfm crosscheck` command verifies that the remote MEP (Maintenance End Point) is properly configured. In Juniper, this is implicitly handled when you configure a remote MEP under a specific MEP in the connectivity-fault-management hierarchy. The command establishes the relationship between the local MEP and its remote counterpart for CFM operations.\\n\\nNote that Juniper's CFM configuration is more hierarchical and explicit compared to Cisco's, requiring you to specify the maintenance domain, association, and MEP levels when configuring remote MEP relationships.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols oam ethernet connectivity-fault-management maintenance-domain md1 maintenance-association ma1 mep 1 remote-mep 2'}]]": 1,
